{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radical of an Ideal.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radical of an Ideal.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radical of an ideal radical of an ideal","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Radical of an Ideal.md","fileName":"Radical of an Ideal.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Radical of an Ideal","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 19:40\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra","Ideal","Prime Ideal","Radical Ideal","Nilradical","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radical of an Ideal.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radical of an Ideal.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radical of an ideal radical of an ideal","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Radical of an Ideal.md","fileName":"Radical of an Ideal.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Radical of an Ideal","heading":"Radical of an Ideal","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The radical √I of an [[Ideal]] I consists of all ring elements r for which some positive power rⁿ lies in I. It is itself an ideal and equals the intersection of all [[Prime Ideal]]s containing I.\n\nTaking a radical removes information about powers while preserving the same prime support. An ideal equal to its radical is a [[Radical Ideal]], and the radical of the zero ideal is the [[Nilradical]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra","Ideal","Prime Ideal","Radical Ideal","Nilradical","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radical of an Ideal.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radical of an Ideal.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radical of an ideal